title: CWPdoor Trojanized cwpsrv Payload Pulled from Staging IP
id: 5f600678-e64c-49e0-9aa1-ea62a18893e1
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects the distribution step of CWPdoor, a passive backdoor embedded in a
    recompiled cwpsrv binary (Control Web Panel 1.15.10 and 1.24.0). XLab
    captured a script posing as the panel updater, cwpsrv_update.sh, which
    downloads the trojanized binary from 136.0.141.71 and overwrites
    /usr/local/cwpsrv/bin/cwpsrv, then restores file timestamps, deletes
    temporary files and wipes the command-history log. The rule fires on the
    updater script name or on any command line that references the staging IP,
    both of which precede the binary swap. A hit means the CWP panel on that
    host should be treated as compromised and rebuilt from clean media.
references:
    - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hackers-backdoor-linux-servers-using-trojanized-cwp-web-server/
    - https://blog.xlab.qianxin.com/beware-of-cwpdoor-the-zero-detection-pas-backdoor-hiding-in-centos-websive-panel/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/06
tags:
    - attack.t1505
    - attack.t1070
    - attack.t1573
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: linux
detection:
    selection_updater:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - 'cwpsrv_update.sh'
    selection_staging:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - '136.0.141.71'
    condition: selection_updater or selection_staging
falsepositives:
    - Legitimate Control Web Panel maintenance that runs a vendor-supplied script of the same name
    - Analysts or scanners deliberately resolving the reported staging IP during incident triage
level: high
